<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Fa23-Midterm1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 - Midterm 1 - Fall 2023</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the lines given by
				<me>
					\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}
				</me>
				and
				<me>
					\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
				</me>
				Find the equation of the plane that contains both lines.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\vec{v}_1 \amp = \langle 0, 1, -1 \rangle \\
					\vec{v}_2 \amp = \langle 2, 1, 3 \rangle \\
					\vec{n} \amp = \vec{v}_1 \times \vec{v}_2 = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \end{vmatrix} = \langle 4, -2, -2 \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				A point on the plane is <m>(4, 3, 1)</m>. The equation of the plane is:
				<me>4(x-4) - 2(y-3) - 2(z-1) = 0.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the surface <m>x+y^2=z^2</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the traces of the form <m>x=k</m>, with <m>k=0,1</m> in the <m>yz</m>-plane where the <m>y</m> axis is horizontal. Label your traces.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						k \amp = 0 \implies z^2 = y^2 \implies z = \pm y \\
						k \amp = 1 \implies z^2 = y^2 + 1 \text{ (hyperbola)}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the traces of the form <m>y=k</m>, with <m>k=0,1</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane where the <m>x</m> axis is horizontal. Label your traces.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						k \amp = 0 \implies z^2 = x \\
						k \amp = 1 \implies z^2 = x + 1
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the traces of the form <m>z=k</m>, with <m>k=0,1</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane where the <m>x</m> axis is horizontal. Label your traces.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						k \amp = 0 \implies x = -y^2 \\
						k \amp = 1 \implies x = 1 - y^2
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the surfaces <m>z=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and <m>z=y+1</m>. Find a vector function that represents the curve of intersection of the given surfaces.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					y+1 \amp = \sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\
					(y+1)^2 \amp = x^2+y^2 \\
					y^2+2y+1 \amp = x^2+y^2 \\
					2y+1 \amp = x^2 \implies y = \frac{x^2-1}{2} \\
					z \amp = y+1 = \frac{x^2+1}{2} \\
					\vec{r}(x) \amp = \langle x, \frac{x^2-1}{2}, \frac{x^2+1}{2} \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the curve given by <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle\sin (\pi t), t^2, 5 t\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the length of the curve, with <m>0 \leq t \leq 2 \pi</m>. You do NOT need to evaluate the integral.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle \pi \cos(\pi t), 2t, 5 \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{\pi^2 \cos^2(\pi t) + 4t^2 + 25} \\
						L \amp = \int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{\pi^2 \cos^2(\pi t) + 4t^2 + 25} \, dt
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a vector equation for the tangent line to the curve at the point <m>(0,4,10)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}(2) \amp = \langle 0, 4, 10 \rangle \\
						\vec{r}\,'(2) \amp = \langle \pi \cos(2\pi), 4, 5 \rangle = \langle \pi, 4, 5 \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					Tangent Line:
					<me>
						\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 4 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} \pi \\ 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> be a vector function in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Assume that <m>\vec{r}(0)=\langle 1,0,0\rangle</m>. Show that if
					<me>\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}\,'(t)=0 \text{ for all } t,</me>
					then <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> lies on a sphere of radius 1.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					If <m>\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}\,'(t) = 0</m>, then
					<me>\frac{d}{dt} |\vec{r}(t)|^2 = \frac{d}{dt} (\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t)) = 2\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}\,'(t) = 0.</me>
					Thus <m>|\vec{r}(t)|^2 = C</m> for some constant <m>C</m>.
					Since <m>|\vec{r}(0)| = \sqrt{1^2+0^2+0^2} = 1</m>, we have <m>1^2 = C \implies C = 1</m>.
					Therefore <m>|\vec{r}(t)| = 1</m> for all <m>t</m>, meaning the curve lies on a sphere of radius 1.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Show that if <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> lies on a sphere of radius <m>a</m> for all <m>t</m>, then
					<me>\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}\,'(t)=0 \text{ for all } t.</me>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					If <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> lies on a sphere of radius <m>a</m>, then <m>|\vec{r}(t)| = a</m> for all <m>t</m>.
					So <m>\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t) = |\vec{r}(t)|^2 = a^2</m>.
					Differentiating both sides with respect to <m>t</m>:
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\frac{d}{dt} (\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t)) \amp = \frac{d}{dt} (a^2) \\
						2\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = 0 \implies \vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}\,'(t) = 0.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the curve given by <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle\sqrt{3} t, \cos (t), \sin (t)\rangle</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit tangent vector, <m>\vec{T}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle \sqrt{3}, -\sin t, \cos t \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{3 + \sin^2 t + \cos^2 t} = \sqrt{4} = 2 \\
						\vec{T}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{\sin t}{2}, \frac{\cos t}{2} \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit normal vector, <m>\vec{N}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{T}\,'(t) \amp = \langle 0, -\frac{\cos t}{2}, -\frac{\sin t}{2} \rangle \\
						|\vec{T}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{\frac{\cos^2 t + \sin^2 t}{4}} = \frac{1}{2} \\
						\vec{N}(t) \amp = \langle 0, -\cos t, -\sin t \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					By using the formula
					<me>\kappa(t)=\frac{|\vec{T}\,'(t)|}{|\vec{r}\,'(t)|}</me>
					find the curvature at <m>t=\pi</m>, that is, <m>\kappa(\pi)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{T}\,'(\pi) \amp = \langle 0, \frac{1}{2}, 0 \rangle \implies |\vec{T}\,'(\pi)| = \frac{1}{2} \\
						|\vec{r}\,'(\pi)| \amp = 2 \text{ (from constant magnitude)} \\
						\kappa(\pi) \amp = \frac{1/2}{2} = \frac{1}{4}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x, y)=\frac{e^{x-y}}{\sqrt{y-x^2+4}}</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the domain of the function.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need <m>y-x^2+4 > 0 \implies y > x^2-4</m>.
					Domain: <m>\{(x, y) \mid y > x^2-4\}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the domain of the function. Shade the region(s) that are part of the domain. Use dotted lines when sketching a curve that is NOT in the domain, and solid lines when sketching curves that are.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The domain is the region above the parabola <m>y = x^2 - 4</m>, with the parabola being dotted (not included).
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				If the limit exists, compute it (making sure to justify your answer). If the limit does not exist, justify why it doesn't exist. If you use a theorem, state clearly which theorem you are using. Make sure to use correct limit notation.
				<me>\lim_{(x, y) \rightarrow (0,0)} \frac{2 x^2 y}{x^4+3 y^2}</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Along Path 1 <m>y=x</m>:
				<me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x^2(x)}{x^4+3x^2} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x^3}{x^2(x^2+3)} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x}{x^2+3} = 0.</me>
				Along Path 2 <m>y=x^2</m>:
				<me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x^2(x^2)}{x^4+3(x^2)^2} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x^4}{4x^4} = \frac{1}{2}.</me>
				Since limits are different, the limit does not exist.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
